# GeneralsX launcher packaging - the single source of every pinned name and version.
#
# Sourced by lib-gate.sh's callers, so every packager sees the same values. POSIX sh assignments
# only: provision-windows.ps1 cannot source this, so package-launcher-windows.sh reads
# TAURI_CLI_VERSION here and passes it in as a -TauriCliVersion parameter, and
# package-launcher-linux.sh passes it as argv[1]. A version bump is therefore a one-file edit.
#
# GX_PRODUCT, GX_APP_VERSION and GX_BUNDLE_ID must equal tauri.conf.json's productName,
# mainBinaryName, version and identifier. check-subsystem2.sh asserts that they do.
TAURI_CLI_VERSION=2.11.4
GX_PRODUCT='GeneralsX Launcher'
GX_APP_VERSION=0.9.71
GX_BUNDLE_ID=dev.wh1ter0se.generalsx.launcher
#
# Only GX_TARGET_DIR_MACOS is read from here, by the two scripts that run on this Mac. The other
# two are recorded, not consumed: they name paths on hosts that never see this file - what is
# copied over is one standalone .ps1 or .sh, which cannot source a Mac path. Those scripts spell
# the literal themselves (provision-windows.ps1, remote-build-windows.ps1, provision-linux.sh,
# remote-build-linux.sh). Changing a remote target dir means editing the remote script AND the
# line here; this file is where a reader looks first, so leaving it silently wrong is worse than
# the duplication.
# THE MACOS TARGET DIR IS SPACE-CHECKED, NOT VOLUME-CHECKED. This named
# /Volumes/Build/GeneralsX/rust-target, an external scratch SSD physically disconnected on
# 2026-08-20 that is not coming back. A packager that defaults to a dead volume refuses every build
# on the only machine that can produce a macOS bundle, so package-launcher-macos.sh now gates on
# measured free space wherever CARGO_TARGET_DIR points instead of on the identity of one disk.
# $HOME rather than a literal home path: this repository is public-bound and an account name is not
# something to commit.
GX_TARGET_DIR_MACOS="$HOME/gx-rust-target"
GX_TARGET_DIR_WINDOWS='C:\dev\gx\rust-target'
GX_TARGET_DIR_LINUX=/root/gxbuild/rust-target
